<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Neuchâtel</h1><p class="st-meta">1034 CE – 1848 CE (approx.) · capital Neuchâtel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small principality on Lake Neuchâtel ruled for 150 years by the kings of Prussia while also allied to, and later part of, the Swiss Confederacy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> ('new castle') appears in 1011 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy">King Rudolf III of Burgundy</a> gave the castle to his wife; in 1034 it passed with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a> to the Empire. Counts of Neuchâtel ruled from the 12th century, and the county passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-hachberg">Hochberg</a> family and in 1504 to the French house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orleans-longueville">Orléans-Longueville</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county became Protestant under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillaume-farel">Guillaume Farel</a> in 1530 and was closely allied with Bern and other Swiss cantons. Its estates and towns enjoyed liberties. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orleans-longueville">Longueville</a> line ended in 1707, the estates chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia">King Frederick I of Prussia</a> as prince. Watchmaking in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura</a> valleys, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-chaux-de-fonds">La Chaux-de-Fonds</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-locle">Le Locle</a>, became the chief industry.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuchatel">Neuchâtel</a> to Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier">Berthier</a> from 1806 to 1814. In 1815 it became a Swiss canton while remaining a Prussian principality, an anomaly ended by the republican revolution of 1848. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> renounced its claims in 1857.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-neuchatel.jpg" alt="Principality of Neuchâtel" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Tschubby · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Kanton_Neuenburg_Bezirke_1850.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1034 CE – 1848 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Neuchâtel</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marie de Nemours, Frederick I of Prussia, Louis-Alexandre Berthier</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinist Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Neuchâtel, Neuenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1441&amp;focus=principality-of-neuchatel" aria-label="Principality of Neuchâtel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 528 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Neuchâtel lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5163 784L5186 782M5163 784L5300 622M5163 784L5175 785"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5186 782h0M5300 622h0M5175 785h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5163 784h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5163 784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Neuchâtel. <a href="/atlas?year=1441&amp;focus=principality-of-neuchatel">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

